                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 916,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Removes a steady drift from a recording - the best-fitting straight line through it - leaving only the movement around that line. Or, with constant, just removes the average level.\n\nSensors drift; performers walk slowly across the room while they gesture; a breathing signal rises as the sensor warms. A drift swamps everything else: it dominates a spectrum, misleads a mean, defeats a threshold. Taking it away first leaves the movement itself.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.detrend <linear or constant>\nDefault: linear.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe recording (a list or array). Every input is detrended and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, use 0.\n\nremove:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) takes away the best straight line;\nconstant takes away only the average level.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndetrended:\nThe recording with the drift taken away, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_drift subpatcher makes a wave of 3 cycles riding on a steady rise from 0 to 3 (left). Detrended (right), the rise is gone and the wave swings about 0.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.filtfilt high-pass removes slow movement of any shape, not only straight lines. np.polyfit fits a curved trend. np.mean and - remove just the average.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 900,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Removes a steady drift from a recording - the best-fitting straight line through it - leaving only the movement around that line. Or, with constant, just removes the average level.\n\nSensors drift; performers walk slowly across the room while they gesture; a breathing signal rises as the sensor warms. A drift swamps everything else: it dominates a spectrum, misleads a mean, defeats a threshold. Taking it away first leaves the movement itself.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.detrend <linear or constant>\nDefault: linear.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe recording (a list or array). Every input is detrended and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - for a buffer of frames x joints, use 0.\n\nremove:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) takes away the best straight line;\nconstant takes away only the average level.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndetrended:\nThe recording with the drift taken away, the same shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_drift subpatcher makes a wave of 3 cycles riding on a steady rise from 0 to 3 (left). Detrended (right), the rise is gone and the wave swings about 0.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.filtfilt high-pass removes slow movement of any shape, not only straight lines. np.polyfit fits a curved trend. np.mean and - remove just the average."
                },
